</div><div class="ltx_page_content">
<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">Simulating Clinical AI Assistance using Multimodal LLMs: 
<br class="ltx_break"> A Case Study in Diabetic Retinopathy</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Nadim Barakat
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_email"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Email: </span><a href="mailto:nadim.barakat@tufts.edu">nadim.barakat@tufts.edu</a>
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Dana-Farber Cancer Institute &amp; Tufts University School of Medicine
<br class="ltx_break"> and  
<br class="ltx_break">Dana-Farber Cancer Institute, Brigham and Women’s Hospital, &amp; Harvard Medical School

</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">William Lotter
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_email"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Email: </span><a href="mailto:lotterb@ds.dfci.harvard.edu">lotterb@ds.dfci.harvard.edu</a>
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>
</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Diabetic retinopathy (DR) is a leading cause of blindness worldwide, and AI systems can expand access to fundus photography screening. Current FDA-cleared systems primarily provide binary referral outputs, where this minimal output may limit clinical trust and utility. Yet, determining the most effective output format to enhance clinician–AI performance is an empirical challenge that is difficult to assess at scale. We evaluated multimodal large language models (MLLMs) for DR detection and their ability to simulate clinical AI assistance across different output types. Two models were tested on IDRiD and Messidor-2: GPT-4o, a general-purpose MLLM, and MedGemma, an open-source medical model. Experiments included: (1) baseline evaluation, (2) simulated AI assistance with synthetic predictions, and (3) actual AI-to-AI collaboration where GPT-4o incorporated MedGemma outputs. MedGemma outperformed GPT-4o at baseline, achieving higher sensitivity and AUROC, while GPT-4o showed near-perfect specificity but low sensitivity. Both models adjusted predictions based on simulated AI inputs, but GPT-4o’s performance collapsed with incorrect ones, whereas MedGemma remained more stable. In actual collaboration, GPT-4o achieved strong results when guided by MedGemma’s descriptive outputs, even without direct image access (AUROC up to 0.96). These findings suggest MLLMs may improve DR screening pipelines and serve as scalable simulators for studying clinical AI assistance across varying output configurations. Open, lightweight models such as MedGemma may be especially valuable in low-resource settings, while descriptive outputs could enhance explainability and clinician trust in clinical workflows.</p>
  
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Diabetes affects hundreds of millions worldwide, and chronic hyperglycemia damages retinal vessels, leading to diabetic retinopathy (DR) and vision loss <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Teo et al., 2021</a>)</cite>. DR severity is graded on the International Clinical DR scale from 0 to 4 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu et al., 2013</a>)</cite>, where grade 2 (moderate non-proliferative diabetic retinopathy) is the threshold for referral to an ophthalmologist. Because early DR is often asymptomatic, the American Academy of Ophthalmology recommends regular screening, which can be performed with fundus photography <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Flaxel et al., 2020</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">AI-based systems have the potential to expand access to DR screening and support accurate referral decisions <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nørgaard and Grauslund, 2018</a>)</cite>. Several FDA-cleared systems for fundus photography already exist, which output a referable DR prediction rather than a detailed description of findings <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rajesh et al., 2023</a>)</cite>. This minimal output and lack of explainability may limit clinician trust and combined clinician–AI performance <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Khokhar et al., 2025</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The question of what outputs best support clinician–AI interaction extends across medical imaging <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">McNamara et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. Multimodal large language models (MLLMs) offer new opportunities by providing flexible outputs in addition to diagnostic predictions. Early ophthalmology studies using ChatGPT or hybrid classifier–LLM pipelines show promise <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Betzler et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Agnihotri et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, but the optimal clinician–AI collaboration strategy remains unclear. Ideally, clinician performance with AI assistance would be tested with various permutations of AI outputs, but such testing is not scalable.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S1.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.13234v1/DR_Experiments_Methods-1.png" id="S1.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/231;" width="476" height="231" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span>Overview of experimental conditions.
Experiment 1: Baseline performance using only fundus images.
Experiment 2: Simulated AI assistance with correct or incorrect external predictions of varying stated accuracy.
Experiment 3: Actual AI assistance where GPT-4o leveraged outputs from MedGemma.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">In this study, we evaluate MLLMs for diabetic retinopathy detection and as clinician–AI emulators for comparing output types and collaboration strategies. We test two models on IDRiD and Messidor-2: GPT-4o, a general-purpose MLLM, and MedGemma, an open-source medical vision–language model whose ophthalmic validation is currently limited but whose accessibility makes it valuable in low-resource settings. Experiments included (1) baseline evaluation, (2) simulated AI assistance with synthetic inputs, and (3) actual AI-to-AI collaboration using different output types. Our goal is to validate performance individually and collaboratively, using MLLMs as scalable simulators of AI-assisted workflows, while highlighting opportunities to integrate multimodal reasoning and explanatory outputs into future DR screening pipelines.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Methods</h2>

<section id="S2.SSx1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Datasets</h3>

<div id="S2.SSx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SSx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We used two public DR datasets of fundus images with expert-graded labels.
IDRiD originates from a clinic in India and includes 103 test images <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Porwal et al., 2018</a>)</cite>, all included in our study.
Messidor-2 originates from multiple ophthalmology departments in France <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Decencière et al., 2014</a>; <a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Abràmoff et al., 2013</a>)</cite>, and was subsampled to produce a balanced set of 175 images (35 per grade 0–4) for evaluation. All images were resized to 512x512.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SSx2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Models</h3>

<div id="S2.SSx2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SSx2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">GPT-4o (model: <span id="S2.SSx2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">gpt-4o-2024-08-06</span>; <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">OpenAI (2024)</a></cite>) was accessed programmatically through the OpenAI API with temperature set to 0; all other parameters were left at their default API values. MedGemma (4B instruction-tuned variant, 4b-it; <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sellergren et al. (2025)</a></cite>) was run locally on an Apple M2 Pro MacBook using the <span id="S2.SSx2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">llama.cpp</span> backend, with all default hyperparameters. Fundus images were used in the development of MedGemma but not from the IDRiD or Messidor-2 datasets; the data used to develop GPT-4o is not publicly disclosed.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SSx3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Experimental Design</h3>

<div id="S2.SSx3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SSx3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluated three conditions to assess performance and the effect of simulated or actual AI assistance.
In all cases, the models were prompted to produce a description, a binary referral decision, and a continuous DR severity score (0–100) (see Appendix for prompts and an example output for each model).</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SSx3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SSx3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SSx3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Baseline:</span> GPT-4o and MedGemma were provided with the fundus images and no additional inputs.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SSx3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SSx3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SSx3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Simulated AI assistance:</span> The baseline prompt was augmented with synthetic input from a simulated AI: <span id="S2.SSx3.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">"To assist your prediction, you are provided with the prediction from another AI model with sensitivity and specificity of Y%. The model’s binary referral prediction is Z."</span>
We varied <math id="S2.SSx3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y\in\{70,80,90\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>70</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>80</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>90</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y\in\{70,80,90\}</annotation></semantics></math> and set <math id="S2.SSx3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> to either the correct or incorrect label.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SSx3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SSx3.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SSx3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Actual AI assistance:</span> We tested whether GPT-4o could leverage real outputs from MedGemma.
Two settings were considered: (<span id="S2.SSx3.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">i</span>) with image, where GPT-4o was given the fundus image plus MedGemma’s binary referral, descriptive text, or both; and (<span id="S2.SSx3.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">ii</span>) without image, where only MedGemma’s descriptive text for each image was provided.
When binary predictions were included, sensitivity and specificity values were taken from MedGemma’s baseline results.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SSx4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Performance Metrics</h3>

<div id="S2.SSx4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SSx4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Ground truth referable labels were derived from the datasets’ DR grades, with referable DR defined as grade <math id="S2.SSx4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\geq" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>≥</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\geq</annotation></semantics></math> 2.
Evaluation metrics included the area under the receiver operating characteristic curve (AUROC) based on the continuous severity score and the binary ground truth, and sensitivity and specificity based on the model’s binary referral output.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Results</h2>

<section id="S3.SSx1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Baseline Performance</h3>

<figure id="S3.T1" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 1: </span>Baseline performance of GPT-4o and MedGemma on IDRiD and Messidor-2 datasets.</figcaption>
<table id="S3.T1.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="S3.T1.2.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S3.T1.2.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_th_row ltx_border_t"><span id="S3.T1.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Dataset</span></th>
<th id="S3.T1.2.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_th_row ltx_border_t"><span id="S3.T1.2.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Model</span></th>
<th id="S3.T1.2.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_t"><span id="S3.T1.2.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Sens.</span></th>
<th id="S3.T1.2.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_t"><span id="S3.T1.2.1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Spec.</span></th>
<th id="S3.T1.2.1.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_t"><span id="S3.T1.2.1.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">AUC</span></th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="S3.T1.2.2" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S3.T1.2.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_t">IDRiD</th>
<th id="S3.T1.2.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_t">GPT-4o</th>
<td id="S3.T1.2.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">0.50</td>
<td id="S3.T1.2.2.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">1.00</td>
<td id="S3.T1.2.2.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">0.78</td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T1.2.3" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S3.T1.2.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row">IDRiD</th>
<th id="S3.T1.2.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row">MedGemma</th>
<td id="S3.T1.2.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">0.92</td>
<td id="S3.T1.2.3.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">0.41</td>
<td id="S3.T1.2.3.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">0.87</td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T1.2.4" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S3.T1.2.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row">Messidor</th>
<th id="S3.T1.2.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row">GPT-4o</th>
<td id="S3.T1.2.4.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">0.40</td>
<td id="S3.T1.2.4.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">1.00</td>
<td id="S3.T1.2.4.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">0.75</td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T1.2.5" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S3.T1.2.5.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_b">Messidor</th>
<th id="S3.T1.2.5.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_b">MedGemma</th>
<td id="S3.T1.2.5.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_b">0.95</td>
<td id="S3.T1.2.5.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_b">0.76</td>
<td id="S3.T1.2.5.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_b">0.95</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SSx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SSx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">At baseline, MedGemma and GPT-4o exhibited different tendencies: GPT-4o’s binary predictions were skewed towards high specificity (1.00) but low sensitivity (0.50 in IDRiD, 0.40 on Messidor-2); MedGemma was highly sensitive (0.92-0.95) with low specificity on IDRiD (0.41) and more balanced specificity on Messidor-2 (0.76). MedGemma outperformed GPT-4o in terms of AUROC on both datasets (Table <a href="#S3.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ Baseline Performance ‣ 3 Results ‣ Simulating Clinical AI Assistance using Multimodal LLMs: A Case Study in Diabetic Retinopathy" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>), with performance on Messidor-2 (AUROC of 0.95) that approaches leading traditional models (AUROC <math id="S3.SSx1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sim" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∼</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sim</annotation></semantics></math>0.97-0.99 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gulshan et al., 2016</a>; <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Papadopoulos et al., 2021</a>)</cite>).</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F2" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.13234v1/updated_simulated_AI_summary_plot.svg" id="S3.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:381/191;" width="381" height="191"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2: </span>Performance under simulated AI assistance.
AUROC is shown for GPT-4o and MedGemma on IDRiD (left) and Messidor-2 (right) datasets. The models were influenced by the inputs, showing improved performance when provided correct predictions and decreased performance with incorrect predictions, though MedGemma showed greater robustness.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S3.SSx2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Simulated AI Assistance</h3>

<div id="S3.SSx2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SSx2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The simulated AI assistance results are shown in Figure <a href="#S3.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ Baseline Performance ‣ 3 Results ‣ Simulating Clinical AI Assistance using Multimodal LLMs: A Case Study in Diabetic Retinopathy" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>.
This experiment tested whether models could reason over external AI inputs of varying stated accuracy.
Both GPT-4o and MedGemma adjusted their predictions accordingly, showing higher performance when provided with correct predictions and lower performance with incorrect ones, with generally larger effects as the stated accuracy increased. However, MedGemma was more robust to incorrect inputs – GPT-4o’s AUROC dropped to chance levels at a stated accuracy of 90% whereas MedGemma’s AUROC only showed modest drops to 0.76 on IDRiD and 0.87 on Messidor-2. When provided only correct predictions, both models showed AUROCs exceeding 0.95 on both datasets.
Full sensitivity and specificity tables are provided in the Appendix.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SSx3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Actual AI Assistance</h3>

<div id="S3.SSx3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SSx3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Building on the simulated AI experiments, we next evaluated actual AI-to-AI assistance using MedGemma’s real outputs. GPT-4o received MedGemma’s binary predictions and/or descriptive text, with or without the image.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SSx3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SSx3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">As shown in Table <a href="#S3.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ Actual AI Assistance ‣ 3 Results ‣ Simulating Clinical AI Assistance using Multimodal LLMs: A Case Study in Diabetic Retinopathy" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, GPT-4o benefited more from MedGemma’s descriptive outputs than from its binary ones. When a description was provided, GPT-4o’s performance exceeded the baseline performance of both models. Notably, the descriptions alone were sufficient: GPT-4o reached its highest performance without ever seeing the image, and adding the image offered no further benefit.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.T2" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 2: </span>GPT-4o performance when provided with varying MedGemma outputs.</figcaption>
<table id="S3.T2.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
