</tbody>
</table>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Discussion</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluated MedGemma and GPT-4o for diabetic retinopathy detection across two benchmark datasets. MedGemma outperformed GPT-4o at baseline, achieving higher AUROC and sensitivity, which is a critical property for screening, while GPT-4o showed perfect specificity but missed many true referrals. Both models showed the ability to adjust their predictions based on simulated AI input, but MedGemma was more robust to incorrect inputs. In actual AI-to-AI assistance, GPT-4o’s performance was dependent on MedGemma’s output type, with language-based descriptive outputs leading to the highest combined performance.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">These findings carry several implications for clinical use. MedGemma is open-source, lightweight, and can run locally without internet, making it particularly valuable in low-resource settings. Current FDA-cleared systems primarily provide binary outputs; richer descriptive outputs could improve explainability, clinician trust, and adoption <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sadeghi et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Khokhar et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. Vision-language models developed specifically for ophthalmology may also provide similar benefits <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shi et al., 2025</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Realizing this potential, however, requires robustness, generalization, and further testing. Prior work highlights vulnerabilities of medical LLMs to adversarial prompts and misinformation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Han et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Thirunavukarasu et al., 2023</a>)</cite>. In our experiments, MedGemma was more resilient than GPT-4o when exposed to misleading predictions. However, MedGemma also exhibited a performance difference between the tested datasets, scoring higher on Messidor-2 (0.95 AUROC) than IDRiD (0.87 AUROC). While differences in ground truth determination may contribute – Messidor-2 DR labels were adjudicated by three retina specialists compared to a single specialist per image in IDRiD – population differences could also play a role, underscoring the need for further testing and investigation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Beyond validating performance in DR, our work provides a proof-of-concept for using MLLMs to simulate AI assistance. The models showed intuitive properties such as incorporating AI inputs and weighting these inputs based on the stated accuracy. GPT-4o’s performance also varied according to the AI input type. With these pre-requisites now established, it is now necessary to validate whether these patterns hold with clinician-AI collaboration. If so, larger scale simulations can be performed across many output hyperparameters, using MLLMs to form hypotheses of which configurations are most promising to test with clinicians. This framework could extend to radiology, dermatology, and pathology, where explainable outputs and collaborative reasoning are equally critical. Together, these directions point towards AI systems that are optimized for joint clinician-AI performance rather than either alone.</p>
</div>
<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
</section>
